00:12:28,420 --> 00:12:38,040
So what we do, we make use of the integrity measurement architecture, IMA, of the kernel.

147
00:12:38,040 --> 00:12:42,540
This is mainline in the meantime.

148
00:12:42,540 --> 00:12:47,920
And the IMA contains a hash of every file.

149
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:54,760
So the contents of every file is hashed and then stored onto your disk or your flash chip

150
00:12:54,760 --> 00:13:02,080
or your media in an extended attribute, it's a security IMA attribute.

151
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:08,280
So far, if you change the contents of the file, you can detect modification of that

152
00:13:08,280 --> 00:13:14,200
file because the signature doesn't match or the hash doesn't match.

153
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:21,680
When the kernel accesses a file, open execute or memap or whatever, it will load the extended

154
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:28,920
attribute, hash the file, and see if the hashes are the same. So, it can detect modification

155
00:13:28,920 --> 00:13:35,780
of it. First step. But if an attacker has access to the system, it can modify a file

156
00:13:35,780 --> 00:13:42,400
and just recalculate the hash and write it to the media. But we want to protect it. We

157
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:47,700
don't want to give the attacker a chance to do this. So, the next step is to make use

158
00:13:47,700 --> 00:13:53,060
of the extended verification module in the kernel, EVM.

159
00:13:53,060 --> 00:14:01,040
So we create a signature over all security attributes.

160
00:14:01,040 --> 00:14:05,720
And this is done on your development PC during creation of the image.

161
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:14,860
So you take, again, your private key, create your root file system, sign every file, every

162
00:14:14,860 --> 00:14:25,360
extended attribute which contains a hash and so you can be sure that the file has not been

163
00:14:25,360 --> 00:14:35,040
modified and the checksum of the file has not been modified. And the EVM has to be verified

164
00:14:35,040 --> 00:14:41,220
against a public key that comes with the kernel from the first stage. So it looks like this

165
00:14:41,220 --> 00:14:47,580
in the overview. The kernel has a public key. The public key verifies the EVM signature.

166
00:14:47,580 --> 00:14:53,460
The EVM signature verifies the IMA hash and the IMA hash verifies the contents of the

167
00:14:53,460 --> 00:15:03,420
file. So, if you boot in our kernel with a so far trusted and verified system, we can

168
00:15:03,420 --> 00:15:09,480
see if this EVM signature is unmodified and then see if this hash is unmodified and then

169
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:17,100
see if the file itself, the contents of the file itself has not been modified. This is

170
00:15:17,100 --> 00:15:25,860
the first step. But if you wanted to read right, you cannot recalculate a RSA signature

171
00:15:25,860 --> 00:15:31,520
because the RSA keys, the private keys, are obviously not on the system. If they were,

172
00:15:31,520 --> 00:15:38,680
you can, as an attacker, just take the private key from the embedded system, recalculate

173
00:15:38,680 --> 00:15:44,420
everything and you're in the system, no one will detect it.

174
00:15:44,420 --> 00:15:50,960
So what EVM then does is make use of a SHA-HMAC.

175
00:15:50,960 --> 00:15:56,440
This is a clever way of hashing things.

176
00:15:56,440 --> 00:16:06,600
And you can then guarantee integrity and authentication of contents.

177
00:16:06,600 --> 00:16:11,540
But you need a different shared secret for this,

178
00:16:11,540 --> 00:16:15,760
for each system, because if the Etika opens one system,

179
00:16:15,760 --> 00:16:20,760
does a modification, and the HMAC gets used,

180
00:16:21,320 --> 00:16:24,640
you don't want, the Etika can transfer a modified file

181
00:16:24,640 --> 00:16:27,020
from one system to another system.

182
00:16:28,640 --> 00:16:32,500
So you need a different shared secret

183
00:16:32,500 --> 00:16:34,460
for the HMAC on every system.

184
00:16:34,460 --> 00:16:41,460
And we do this on the IMX6 and I tell you later how it is done.

185
00:16:41,460 --> 00:16:56,460
And the funniest thing is an HMAC, because it's just a bunch of hashes, can be verified faster than an RSA operation.

186
00:16:56,460 --> 00:17:01,460
This is preferred if you want to boot fast.

187
00:17:01,460 --> 00:17:06,460
Once the kernel opens every file or touches a file,

188
00:17:07,700 --> 00:17:12,700
it will recalculate HMAC-based verification

189
00:17:13,740 --> 00:17:16,980
and write it down because it's much more faster

190
00:17:16,980 --> 00:17:18,400
than an ARIS-A1.

191
00:17:19,800 --> 00:17:24,600
And the Attica cannot calculate a new HMAC

192
00:17:24,600 --> 00:17:29,600
when he has not access to the SQL that is used in the EVM.

193
00:17:31,460 --> 00:17:42,900
how we do this. So, we have our SOC that has been fused, and on the IMX6, you have access,

194
00:17:43,260 --> 00:17:48,560
if you have booted your bootloader correctly signed, you have access to a unique key that

195
00:17:48,560 --> 00:17:56,900
is unique for every IMX6. So, you cannot transfer data with it encrypted from one system to

196
00:17:56,900 --> 00:18:05,180
another. This only works if you boot your system with a secured and verified bootloader.

